talk(534,'1981-09-28','Benedict Seifert','P000985-P000990','Transformation-group geometry and a Bruhat-type decomposition',r'''The actual heading is on P000987 and includes a question mark after the Russian surname and the affiliation Paris. Fixed sets of pointwise stabilizers define a closure geometry; the speaker builds flags, proposes four axioms and asserts a double-coset decomposition. The closest publication is Seifert's \emph{Transitivity Properties, Bruhat Decomposition and Tits Systems for Transformation Groups}, received 5 March 1983, published June 1984. Its exact axiom list could not be read in this run, so the identification is partial. Seifert's later paper explicitly distinguishes its new tree transitivity and proof from the earlier work \citep[Introduction and reference S.1]{seifert1986highly}. Gelfand's three concrete double-coset exercises occur on P000989 before the Zelevinsky heading.''')

item(534,'P000987','definition','Closed sets from pointwise stabilizers',
 r'''For an action \(G\curvearrowright X\), \(S\) belongs to \(\mathcal F\) when every \(x\notin S\) can be moved by some group element fixing every point of \(S\). Arbitrary intersections of members of \(\mathcal F\) are closed, and the intersection of all closed supersets defines \(S^{\mathrm{cl}}\).''',
 r'''Write \(G_{(S)}=\{g:gs=s\text{ for every }s\in S\}\), and \(\operatorname{Fix}(H)=\{x:hx=x\text{ for every }h\in H\}\). Then \(\mathcal F=\{S:S=\operatorname{Fix}(G_{(S)})\}\), and \(\operatorname{cl}(S)=\operatorname{Fix}(G_{(S)})\). This operation is extensive, monotone, idempotent and equivariant under \(G\); intersections of closed sets are closed. The bottom is \(\operatorname{cl}(\varnothing)=\operatorname{Fix}(G)\), which need not be empty, and the top is \(X\).''',
 r'''These assertions are elementary consequences of the order-reversing stabilizer/fixed-set correspondence: \(S\subseteq\operatorname{Fix}(H)\) iff \(H\subseteq G_{(S)}\). Their particular use in Seifert's Bruhat program is linked to \citet{seifert1984transitivity} by \citet[Introduction and reference S.1]{seifert1986highly}, but the formal closure argument needs no transitivity or finiteness assumption.''',
 ['seifert1984transitivity','seifert1986highly'],reconstruction='The closure formula and bottom element make the source’s terminal (0) notation precise. They are elementary reconstruction of the displayed definition, not an inferred extra axiom.',literal='correct',match='partial',verification='indirect',area='Transformation groups and incidence geometry')

item(534,'P000987','construction','Setwise stabilizers and the restriction exact sequence',
 r'''The notebook distinguishes \(G_S=\bigcap_{s\in S}G_s\) from \(G^S=\{g:gS=S\}\), and gives an extension by a barred permutation group on \(S\).''',
 r'''For every subset \(S\subseteq X\), restriction induces an exact sequence \[1\longrightarrow G_{(S)}\longrightarrow G_{\{S\}}\longrightarrow G_{\{S\}}|_S\longrightarrow1,\] where \(G_{\{S\}}\) is the setwise stabilizer and the right-hand group is the actual image in \(\operatorname{Sym}(S)\). The image need not be every automorphism of an independently defined geometry on \(S\).''',
 r'''This is the first-isomorphism theorem for the restriction homomorphism. It is the exact algebraic content of the notebook's extension diagram; the later Seifert citation provides context, not an additional surjectivity onto a larger group \citep[Introduction]{seifert1986highly}.''',
 ['seifert1986highly'],literal='correct',match='partial',verification='indirect',area='Transformation groups and incidence geometry')

item(534,'P000987','construction','Ordered and saturated flags in the closure geometry',
 r'''For a partially ordered set \(I\), the speaker considers maps \(f:I\to\mathcal F\) satisfying \(f(i)\subseteq f(j)\iff i\le j\), and then requires adjacent elements of \(I\) to map to adjacent closed sets.''',
 r'''The first condition says that \(f\) is an order embedding. The second is preservation of covering relations: if \(i<j\) with no intermediate element, there is no closed set strictly between \(f(i)\) and \(f(j)\). Equivariance of closure gives \((g\cdot f)(i)=g f(i)\), an action on both families. When \(I\) is a chain and the image is maximal, its stabilizer is \(B_f=\bigcap_iG_{\{f(i)\}}\). These constructions alone assert neither flag transitivity nor a Bruhat decomposition.''',
 r'''This is a precise formalization of the displayed definitions. Seifert's subsequently published transformation-group program concerns additional transitivity hypotheses; his 1986 Introduction explicitly says that its tree formulation is different from the 1984 approach \citep{seifert1986highly}.''',
 ['seifert1986highly'],literal='correct',match='partial',verification='indirect',area='Transformation groups and incidence geometry')

item(534,'P000988','claim','Singleton closures and an incorrect stabilizer-equality assertion',
 r'''For a transitive action, the closure of \(\{x\}\) is called a line and is said to be the set of points with \ru{тем же стабилизатором}, the same stabilizer as \(x\).''',
 r'''Always \(\operatorname{cl}\{x\}=\{y:G_x\subseteq G_y\}\). Equality of stabilizers follows, for example, when \(G\) is finite and transitive, because conjugate stabilizers then have the same finite order. It is false for arbitrary infinite transitive actions. Let \(G=\mathbb Z[1/2]\rtimes\langle t\rangle\), with \(tbt^{-1}=2b\), and let \(H=\mathbb Z\) be the translation subgroup. In the transitive action on left cosets \(G/H\), the points \(x=H\) and \(y=t^{-1}H\) have stabilizers \(H\) and \(t^{-1}Ht=\tfrac12\mathbb Z\), respectively. Thus \(G_x\subsetneq G_y\), so \(y\in\operatorname{cl}\{x\}\) but their stabilizers differ.''',
 r'''The corrected inclusion formula follows directly from the definition in 1981-534.1. The displayed affine-group counterexample checks all hypotheses of the literal claim and shows that transitivity alone cannot justify equality. This audit concerns the notebook; it does not allege that the inaccessible 1984 paper contains the same error \citep{seifert1984transitivity}.''',
 ['seifert1984transitivity'],reconstruction='The infinite affine-group example and the finite-action repair are editorial checks. No exact published version of this sentence has been verified.',literal='false as written',match='none found',verification='indirect',area='Transformation groups and incidence geometry')

item(534,'P000988','theorem','Double transitivity and its singleton-closure obstruction',
 r'''Double transitivity is identified with transitivity on \((F\times F)\setminus\Delta\) and with transitivity of a point stabilizer on the remaining points. A non-singleton singleton closure is said to prevent double transitivity.''',
 r'''For a transitive action on a set \(F\) with at least two points, 2-transitivity is equivalent to transitivity of \(G_x\) on \(F\setminus\{x\}\). If \(|F|\ge3\), a 2-transitive action has \(\operatorname{Fix}(G_x)=\{x\}\), so a larger singleton closure obstructs it. The condition \(|F|\ge3\) matters: the regular action of the group of order two on two points is 2-transitive, but \(G_x=1\) and \(\operatorname{cl}\{x\}=F\). Alternatively, the source's pictured obstruction is valid whenever \(\{x\}\subsetneq\operatorname{cl}\{x\}\subsetneq F\).''',
 r'''These are immediate orbit-stabilizer criteria; they explain precisely the argument in the notebook's three-point drawing. Seifert's later paper makes multiple transitivity a structural input rather than a consequence of single transitivity \citep[Introduction and Definition 2.1]{seifert1986highly}. The two-point counterexample is an editorial boundary check.''',
 ['seifert1986highly'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',area='Transformation groups and incidence geometry')

item(534,'P000988-P000989','claim','Four geometric axioms and the asserted Bruhat decomposition',
 r'''The axioms are transitivity on maximal flags; finite flag dimension; a dimension-intersection condition; and surjectivity of restriction to \(S\) for elements also preserving a disjoint singleton closure. They are followed by \(G=\coprod_WG^fwG^f\).''',
 r'''Here is the strongest literal formulation, with normalizations made explicit. Put \(0=\operatorname{Fix}(G)\). Assume maximal flags are a single \(G\)-orbit; the rank \(r(S)\) is finite and given by the length of maximal chains from \(0\) to \(S\); if \(S,S'\subseteq T\) and \(r(S)+r(S')>r(T)\), then \(S\cap S'\ne0\); and whenever \(C=\operatorname{cl}\{x\}\) has \(C\cap S=0\), restriction \(G_{\{S\}}\cap G_{\{C\}}\to G_{\{S\}}|_S\) is onto. The notebook claims that for a maximal flag with stabilizer \(B\) and its frame group \(N\), representatives of \(W=N/T\) give distinct double cosets \(G=\coprod_{w\in W}B\dot wB\).''',
 r'''The author, Paris location and subject fit \citet{seifert1984transitivity}, received 5 March 1983 and published June 1984, and Seifert's own \citet[Introduction, reference S.1]{seifert1986highly} confirms the predecessor. The 1984 full text was not accessible: its hypotheses and proof have not been checked against these four lines. The 1986 tree theorem is a different theorem and is not substituted for them. Classification A records the credible publication lineage; the publication match remains partial.''',
 ['seifert1984transitivity','seifert1986highly'],cls='A',reconstruction='Rank normalization, the meaning of the restriction image, and representatives dot-w are editorial. The displayed assertion remains an attributed notebook claim; no proof is certified here solely from the abridged axioms.',literal='not determinable',match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',area='Transformation groups and incidence geometry',status='As of November 2026, the publication lineage is identified, but exact theorem-level verification of the 1984 axiom system remains an access/identification limitation. It is not classified as an unresolved mathematical conjecture.')

item(534,'P000989','construction','Frames, their normalizer and the Weyl quotient',
 r'''Every maximal flag is said to have \(f(i)=\operatorname{cl}\{e_j:j\le i\}\). Put \(C_i=\operatorname{cl}\{e_i\}\); \(N\) permutes these sets and \(T\) preserves each one. An exact sequence ends in \(W=N/T\).''',
 r'''For a chosen finite family of distinct closed sets \(\mathcal C=\{C_1,\ldots,C_r\}\), let \(N\) be its setwise stabilizer and \(T=\bigcap_iG_{\{C_i\}}\). The permutation action gives \(1\to T\to N\to W\to1\), with \(W\) its image in \(S_r\). This exact sequence is automatic. The additional statement that every maximal flag admits such a point-generated frame, and that this particular \(W\) parametrizes all \(B\)-double cosets, belongs to the preceding claimed geometric theorem; it is not true for an arbitrary closure operator without extra hypotheses.''',
 r'''The quotient construction is elementary, while its Bruhat role matches Seifert's 1984 program only partially verified through \citet[Introduction and reference S.1]{seifert1986highly}. The notes do not justify importing all Coxeter or BN-pair axioms from the word `Weyl'; the 1986 paper itself exhibits a modified Tits-system setting.''',
 ['seifert1984transitivity','seifert1986highly'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',area='Transformation groups and incidence geometry',status='As of November 2026, the exact sequence is fully determined by its definition. The asserted frame theorem remains tied to the incompletely verified 1984 theorem, rather than a new open problem.')

item(534,'P000989','remark','Unspecified examples outside the axioms',
 r'''The speaker knows no examples other than classical ones satisfying all axioms, but says that some groups have a Bruhat decomposition while failing the axioms.''',
 r'''The first sentence records the speaker's knowledge at that date. The second asserts the existence of a group/action with a double-coset decomposition of the indicated kind that fails at least one displayed axiom, but it supplies no group, action or failed axiom. Consequently no exact example can be identified from this line.''',
 r'''\citet[Introduction and Theorem 2.2]{seifert1986highly} later supplies concrete tree-action examples with a modified Tits system and Bruhat decomposition. That is relevant thematic development, but its deliberately different transitivity framework does not identify the unnamed 1981 examples.''',
 ['seifert1986highly'],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='read the result',confidence='low',area='Transformation groups and incidence geometry',status='As of November 2026, the unnamed examples remain unidentified; neither the historical knowledge claim nor failure of the particular 1981 axioms is inferred from later thematic examples.')

dc1=item(534,'P000989','problem','Unitary double cosets and singular values',
 r'''Gelfand's first exercise is to describe \(U(n)\backslash GL(n)/U(n)\); neither the field nor \(U(n)\) is expanded in the surviving line.''',
 r'''Under the natural unitary interpretation \(G=GL_n(\mathbb C)\), \(U(n)\) the unitary group, each double coset has exactly one representative \(\operatorname{diag}(s_1,\ldots,s_n)\) with \(s_1\ge\cdots\ge s_n>0\). These are the singular values: diagonalize the positive Hermitian matrix \(g^*g\), take its positive square root, and absorb the two unitary factors. Thus the quotient is the closed positive Weyl chamber in \(\mathbb R_{>0}^n\).''',
 r'''This is the classical singular-value/polar-decomposition calculation, not an open problem. The proof above supplies the precise quotient directly. Compact/complex group comparison is reviewed in \citet[VIII.7]{serre1987complex}; that citation is contextual, not a claim that this exact exercise originates there.''',
 ['serre1987complex'],reconstruction='The interpretation of U as unitary and the complex ground field are explicit assumptions. If U meant an upper unitriangular subgroup, the answer would be different; the ambiguous symbol is logged.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',problem='GP-1981-02',area='Classical matrix groups')

dc2=item(534,'P000989','problem','Borel double cosets and permutation matrices',
 r'''The second exercise is \(B(n)\backslash GL(n)/B(n)\), with `triangular' written under \(B(n)\).''',
 r'''For \(G=GL_n(k)\) over a field and \(B\) its invertible upper triangular subgroup, \(G=\coprod_{w\in S_n}B\dot wB\), where \(\dot w\) is the permutation matrix. Hence the double-coset set is \(S_n\). Equivalently, two complete flags have a relative-position permutation, determined by the dimensions of intersections of their steps. This should be distinguished from merely counting \(n!\) possible representatives without establishing disjointness.''',
 r'''This is the classical Bruhat decomposition. \citet[VIII.4, Theorems 7--8 and Corollary]{serre1987complex} states the bijection between the Weyl group and Borel double cosets in the complex group setting. It explains the concrete model for Seifert's abstract decomposition.''',
 ['serre1987complex'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',problem='GP-1981-02',area='Classical matrix groups')

dc3=item(534,'P000989','problem','Mixed unitary–Borel double cosets',
 r'''The third exercise is \(U(n)\backslash GL(n)/B(n)\).''',
 r'''For \(GL_n(\mathbb C)\), with \(U(n)\) unitary and \(B\) invertible upper triangular, Gram--Schmidt gives \(g=ur\) with \(u\in U(n)\), \(r\in B\). Thus there is exactly one \(U(n)\)-\(B\) double coset. Equivalently, the unitary group acts transitively on the complex complete flag manifold; the stabilizer of the standard flag in \(U(n)\) is the diagonal compact torus.''',
 r'''This is a pre-existing classical matrix-factorization exercise, with a direct proof by orthonormalizing the columns. The compact-group/complex-flag framework is summarized in \citet[VIII.4 and VIII.7]{serre1987complex}. No additional double-coset invariant survives in this mixed quotient.''',
 ['serre1987complex'],reconstruction='As in the first exercise, unitary U and the complex field are explicit reconstructed conventions.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='indirect',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',problem='GP-1981-02',area='Classical matrix groups')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1981-02',title='Three classical double-coset spaces',statement=r'''Describe \(U(n)\backslash GL_n(\mathbb C)/U(n)\), \(B\backslash GL_n(\mathbb C)/B\), and \(U(n)\backslash GL_n(\mathbb C)/B\), with \(U(n)\) unitary and \(B\) the invertible upper triangular subgroup; the unitary interpretation is explicitly reconstructed.''',occurrences=[dc1,dc2,dc3],status=r'''As of November 2026: classical solved exercises. The invariants are respectively ordered positive singular values, a permutation in \(S_n\), and no invariant (one double coset). The three distinct questions share this problem entry. The earlier Bruhat occurrence 1979-483.1 is a related theorem, not an identical three-part question.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=['serre1987complex']))

