talk(533,'1981-09-21','S. I. Gelfand','P000985-P000987','Verma embeddings and a root-multiplicity question',r'''The return arrow on P000986 begins a review of Verma modules and the Bernstein--Gelfand--Gelfand criterion for embeddings. The formulas mix the notebook's unshifted highest weight with a negative rho shift and do not delimit the finite-dimensional semisimple scope of the quoted theorem. At the top of P000987, Gelfand asks for root-space dimensions and independent words for general Cartan matrices. The next dated heading starts Seifert's talk. The multiplicity question is indexed, but neither efficiency nor a uniform positive combinatorial formula is demanded in the surviving wording; no artificial open conjecture is extracted.''')

item(533,'P000986','construction','Verma modules and their PBW weight structure',
 r'''For \(\lambda\in H^*\), impose \(e_iv_0=0\) and \(hv_0=\lambda(h)v_0\), and apply words in the \(f_i\). The notes calculate the weight \(\lambda-\alpha_{i_1}-\cdots-\alpha_{i_k}\).''',
 r'''For a complex Lie algebra with triangular decomposition \(\mathfrak g=\mathfrak n_-\oplus\mathfrak h\oplus\mathfrak n_+\) of the semisimple or contragredient type under discussion, set \(\mathfrak b_+=\mathfrak h\oplus\mathfrak n_+\), and let \(\mathbb C_\lambda\) be its one-dimensional module with \(\mathfrak n_+\) acting by zero. Then \(M(\lambda)=U(\mathfrak g)\otimes_{U(\mathfrak b_+)}\mathbb C_\lambda\cong U(\mathfrak n_-)\) as a vector space. It is the universal highest-weight module of highest weight \(\lambda\); its weight set is contained in \(\lambda-Q_+\). Arbitrary \(f_i\)-words span, while a PBW basis uses ordered monomials in a basis of \(\mathfrak n_-\).''',
 r'''This construction predates the seminar. \citet[\S3, Definition 1 and Lemma 1]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1971structure} uses a shifted parameter: its module \(M_\xi\) has highest weight \(\xi-\rho\). Keeping that convention separate from the notebook's \(M(\lambda)\) is necessary for the subsequent embedding test. The prior index's 1980-520.1 concerns the same construction but not a previously indexed open question.''',
 ['bernsteingelfandgelfand1971structure'],reconstruction='The induction notation specifies precisely the universal quotient. No additional freeness of arbitrary simple-generator words is inferred.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')

item(533,'P000986','theorem','One-dimensional Hom spaces and injectivity of Verma homomorphisms',
 r'''The notes assert \(\operatorname{Hom}(M(\lambda),M(\mu))=0\) or \(\mathbb C\), and every nonzero map is injective, immediately after introducing \(G(A)\).''',
 r'''For a finite-dimensional complex semisimple Lie algebra and any \(\lambda,\mu\in\mathfrak h^*\), \(\dim\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathfrak g}(M(\lambda),M(\mu))\le1\), and every nonzero homomorphism is an embedding. The image of the highest vector is a singular vector of weight \(\lambda\). Injectivity alone also follows from the freeness over \(U(\mathfrak n_-)\) and the domain property of that enveloping algebra. The one-dimensional bound requires its own theorem and is not inferred merely from PBW.''',
 r'''\citet[\S3, Theorem 1, attributed there to Verma]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1971structure} gives exactly this finite-dimensional semisimple statement. The notebook has not supplied the restrictions needed for an arbitrary Kac--Moody generalization; imaginary-root and critical-weight phenomena cannot be discarded by calling the theorem formal.''',
 ['bernsteingelfandgelfand1971structure'],reconstruction='The verified theorem is deliberately stated in its finite-dimensional semisimple scope. This is not a claim that every assertion fails in every larger class.',match='partial')

item(533,'P000986','theorem','The BGG strong-linkage criterion and the rho-sign error',
 r'''A nonzero homomorphism is said to imply \(\lambda\le\mu\), \(\lambda-\rho=w(\mu-\rho)\), and an ordered sequence of integral reflections. The same three conditions are declared sufficient. The scanned minus signs before both rho terms are clear.''',
 r'''In the preceding finite-dimensional semisimple setting, an embedding \(M(\lambda)\hookrightarrow M(\mu)\) exists exactly when there is a sequence \(\nu_0=\mu,\nu_r=\lambda\), positive roots \(\beta_j\), and integers \(m_j=\langle\nu_{j-1}+\rho,\beta_j^\vee\rangle>0\) with \(\nu_j=s_{\beta_j}(\nu_{j-1}+\rho)-\rho=\nu_{j-1}-m_j\beta_j\), allowing the empty sequence. In particular \(\lambda+\rho=w(\mu+\rho)\); Weyl-orbit membership and the root order alone are weaker than the full ordered condition.''',
 r'''\citet[\S4, condition (A), Theorem 2, and \S5, Theorem 3]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1971structure} proves necessity and sufficiency, using shifted parameters from the outset; the paper was received 5 October 1970 and published in 1971. Under the notebook's explicit unshifted definition, the minus sign is false: for \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\), the singular vector \(fv_0\) gives \(M(-2)\hookrightarrow M(0)\), and \(-2+1=-(0+1)\), whereas \(-2-1\) is not \(\pm(0-1)\).''',
 ['bernsteingelfandgelfand1971structure'],reconstruction='The chain is rewritten with a single forward index to remove the notebook’s dangling delta subscript i-1. Positivity of the roots and integrality at each reflection are explicit. The sl2 test is an editorial verification, not an additional claim from the notes.',literal='false as written',match='exact')

root_occ=item(533,'P000987','problem','Computing root-space dimensions and independent root words',
 r'''Gelfand labels the question \ru{Задача (не банальная)}: for a Cartan matrix satisfying conditions 1 and 2, \ru{Какова размерность пр-ва корней?} The next line mentions constructing linearly independent words for every root.''',
 r'''Given a generalized Cartan matrix \(A\), the maximal contragredient quotient defined in record 532, and a nonzero degree \(\alpha=\sum_i n_i\alpha_i\), determine \(\dim\mathfrak g_\alpha\), and obtain representatives by Lie words when that component is nonzero. The wording does not require a positive combinatorial formula, a canonical basis or a complexity bound. For symmetrizable \(A\), let \(D=\sum_{w\in W}(-1)^{\ell(w)}e^{w\rho-\rho}\) and \(-\log D=\sum_{\gamma>0}c_\gamma e^{-\gamma}\). Denominator inversion gives \(\operatorname{mult}\alpha=\sum_{d\mid\alpha}\mu(d)c_{\alpha/d}/d\), with \(d\mid\alpha\) meaning divisibility of every simple-root coefficient.''',
 r'''\citet[\S2 and Theorem 2]{bermanmoody1979multiplicities}, received 28 September 1978 and published September 1979, already gives an explicit inversion formula for \emph{symmetrizable} Cartan matrices. That hypothesis is not in Gelfand's question. In the wider maximal-quotient definition, each specified degree is nevertheless a finite linear-algebra computation: for height greater than one, \(x\mapsto([f_i,x])_i\) embeds \(\mathfrak g_\alpha\) into the sum of lower positive components. Its rank on a finite free-Lie spanning family computes the multiplicity, and pivot columns select independent words. The annotation proves this elementary recursive observation, rather than attributing a nonsymmetrizable formula to Berman--Moody.''',
 ['bermanmoody1979multiplicities','kac1968graded'],reconstruction='The explicit computational interpretation and the recursive lowering proof are editorial. No request for a uniform cancellation-free or efficient formula is added. The notes may have been seeking something more illuminating than elimination, but that stronger demand is not specified.',cls='E',literal='correct',match='partial',problem='GP-1981-01',status='As of November 2026, the finite computational problem as stated is answerable; symmetrizable multiplicity formulas were available before the meeting. Difficult current questions about positive formulas, growth or complexity are not declared identical to this broad notebook question.',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
