talk(535,'1981-09-28','A. V. Zelevinsky','P000988-P000991','Equioriented type-A quiver orbits and their geometry',r'''The actual exposition begins in the lower part of P000989 and explicitly reports \citet{abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry}, whose September 1981 issue and receipt date of 1 April 1981 precede the meeting. It explains the finite orbit classification, rank invariants, good singularities of orbit closures and the determinantal special case. The closing analogy with Schubert geometry has a precise later realization in Zelevinsky's 1985 paper; the accompanying p-adic multiplicity connection must be distinguished from that purely geometric identification. The next dated heading on P000990 begins Kirillov's talk.''')

item(535,'P000989','construction','The representation variety and base-change action of A_m',
 r'''A chain \(V_1\to\cdots\to V_m\) is called a representation of \(A_m\), and the product of the general linear groups acts. Its last arrow is labelled \(A_m\) although there are only \(m-1\) arrows.''',
 r'''Fix finite-dimensional spaces \(V_i\) over an algebraically closed field \(k\), with \(d_i=\dim V_i\). For the equioriented quiver \(1\to2\to\cdots\to m\), put \(E(\mathbf d)=\prod_{i=1}^{m-1}\operatorname{Hom}(V_i,V_{i+1})\). The group \(G(\mathbf d)=\prod_iGL(V_i)\) acts by \((g\cdot A)_i=g_{i+1}A_i g_i^{-1}\). Its orbits are exactly the isomorphism classes of representations with this fixed dimension vector. The last arrow is \(A_{m-1}:V_{m-1}\to V_m\).''',
 r'''This is the setup of \citet[\S1.1, p.~403]{abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry}, already in print at the time of this explicitly attributed exposition. The orientation is part of the problem, not a cosmetic choice: the particular composition-rank coordinates below use the linear orientation. The earlier finite-quiver discussion 1972-277.8 and GP-1972-301 is related background, rather than an additional unresolved problem here.''',
 ['abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Quiver representations and algebraic geometry')

item(535,'P000989-P000990','theorem','Finite orbit classification by interval multiplicities',
 r'''There are finitely many orbits, an explicit description is possible, and a second description uses multiplicities of occurrences.''',
 r'''Let \(I[p,q]\), \(1\le p\le q\le m\), have one-dimensional spaces at vertices \(p,\ldots,q\), zero spaces elsewhere, and identity maps between adjacent nonzero spaces. Every equioriented \(A_m\)-representation is uniquely a direct sum \(\bigoplus_{p\le q} I[p,q]^{\oplus m_{pq}}\), with \(m_{pq}\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}\). For fixed \(\mathbf d\), the equations \(d_i=\sum_{p\le i\le q}m_{pq}\) permit finitely many such arrays, hence finitely many orbits. It is the fixed-dimension variety, not the collection over all possible dimensions, that has finitely many orbits.''',
 r'''The interval/multiplicity description is explicit in \citet[\S2.3, p.~404]{abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry}. It is the type-A case of the pre-existing finite-type quiver classification. This item specifies what the notebook's abbreviated `occurrences' must mean; it does not attribute a new classification theorem to the reporting speaker.''',
 ['abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry'],reconstruction='The interval modules and dimension equations unpack the notebook’s second classification without changing its fixed-dimension context.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Quiver representations and algebraic geometry')

item(535,'P000990','theorem','Composition ranks, interval inversion and degeneration order',
 r'''The notes prescribe ranks \(r_{ij}\) of all possible compositions and say that a system of inequalities must hold.''',
 r'''Set \(r_{ii}=d_i\) and \(r_{ij}=\operatorname{rank}(A_{j-1}\cdots A_i)\) for \(i<j\), with ranks beyond the end vertices zero. Then \(r_{ij}=\sum_{p\le i\le j\le q}m_{pq}\), and \[m_{ij}=r_{ij}-r_{i-1,j}-r_{i,j+1}+r_{i-1,j+1}.\] Thus the ranks determine the orbit. Rank arrays of representations are precisely the arrays obtained from nonnegative integral \(m_{ij}\) by the first formula, subject to the prescribed diagonal. For two representations of the same dimension vector, \(\mathcal O_B\subseteq\overline{\mathcal O_A}\) if and only if \(r_{ij}(B)\le r_{ij}(A)\) for every \(i\le j\).''',
 r'''\citet[\S2.1, Proposition 2.2 and \S2.3, p.~404]{abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry} supplies the exact invariant and degeneration statements. Upper semicontinuity proves necessity of the rank inequalities; the converse is the nontrivial orbit-closure result, not an automatic consequence of writing down minors. The finite-difference inversion is also verified in the accompanying computation.''',
 ['abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry'],reconstruction='The explicit finite-difference formula and the closure-order converse are read from the identified paper; the notebook only records the ranks and unspecified inequalities. They are labelled here as completion from that source.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Quiver representations and algebraic geometry')

item(535,'P000989','theorem','Normality, Cohen–Macaulayness and rational singularities',
 r'''The orbit closures are called good varieties: normal, Cohen--Macaulay, and so on.''',
 r'''Over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, every orbit closure in the representation space of the equioriented \(A_m\)-quiver is normal and Cohen--Macaulay, and has rational singularities. Here rational singularities means that for a resolution \(\pi:Y\to X\), \(\pi_*\mathcal O_Y=\mathcal O_X\) and \(R^i\pi_*\mathcal O_Y=0\) for \(i>0\). The claim concerns the reduced orbit closure, not an arbitrary scheme defined by equations with the same points.''',
 r'''This is \citet[Theorem 0.2, p.~401; characteristic-zero convention in \S1.1]{abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry}. The published theorem was visually checked in the author's full-text copy. `Rational singularities' is the identified paper's precise addition to the notebook's `etc.', rather than an independently recorded notebook claim. Later scheme-theoretic refinements should be cited separately.''',
 ['abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry'],reconstruction='The field and characteristic are made explicit; rational singularities completes the openly abbreviated list from the paper being reported.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Quiver representations and algebraic geometry')

item(535,'P000990','claim','The Schubert analogy and the Zelevinsky map',
 r'''The quiver orbits are analogous to Schubert cells, with corresponding polynomials.''',
 r'''The exact geometric completion is a locally closed embedding of the equioriented quiver representation space into an appropriate partial flag variety, obtained from a block unitriangular matrix whose blocks are consecutive compositions. Under this map, each orbit closure identifies with a suitable open part of a Schubert variety, with an explicitly matched rank/double-coset index. Consequently local intersection-cohomology polynomials are type-A Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials after the indexing and dimension shifts are fixed. An orbit itself need not be an affine cell: for one map \(\mathbb C\to\mathbb C\), the rank-one orbit is \(\mathbb C^*\).''',
 r'''\citet[Theorem 1 and Corollary 1]{zelevinsky1985graded}, received 4 April 1983 and published in 1985, makes this analogy precise. \citet[\S1.3 and \S2.2]{lakshmibaimagyar1997degeneracy}, circulated in September 1997, strengthens the correspondence to determinantal schemes and explains what the earlier set-theoretic proof leaves to establish. Thus the later exact construction is credited to the speaker, while scheme refinements are credited separately.''',
 ['zelevinsky1985graded','lakshmibaimagyar1997degeneracy'],cls='A',match='partial',reconstruction='The map, flag variety and intersection-cohomology interpretation are the identified later realization of an analogy; they are not literally stated in the 1981 shorthand.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Quiver representations and algebraic geometry',status='As of November 2026, the Schubert-patch description and its scheme-theoretic strengthening are established. The elementary counterexample rules out reading “analogous” as a literal assertion that every quiver orbit is an affine Schubert cell.')

padic_occ=item(535,'P000990','claim','Quiver intersection cohomology and p-adic representation multiplicities',
 r'''The polynomials attached to these varieties are said to describe representations of p-adic groups, in analogy with classical-group representation theory and Schubert geometry. The notes name neither the group nor the multiplicity formula.''',
 r'''The securely identifiable nearby problem is Zelevinsky's 1981 conjecture for smooth finite-length complex representations of \(GL_n(F)\), with \(F\) a nonarchimedean local field. On one fixed cuspidal line, multisegments label standard induced modules \(\pi_a\), irreducibles \(\langle b\rangle\), and orbits \(\mathcal O_a,\mathcal O_b\) in a graded nilpotent/type-A quiver variety with the same dimension vector. With the original submodule convention, the conjecture gives \([\pi_a:\langle b\rangle]=\sum_j\dim\mathcal H^j(IC(\overline{\mathcal O_b}))_{x_a}\), with a consistent shift normalization and \(x_a\in\mathcal O_a\). The associated graded dimensions form a local intersection-cohomology polynomial, evaluated at one for the multiplicity. This is a statement about stalks and Jordan--H\"older factors, not ordinary global cohomology or a classification of all p-adic groups.''',
 r'''\citet[Introduction and \S1.9]{zelevinsky1981padic}, received 27 November 1980 and published April--June 1981, states the conjecture before this seminar; its original Russian full text was read. For the modern standard-quotient normalization, \citet[Theorem 3.9, p.~46, version 2]{balodis2025padic} proves the corresponding \(GL_n\) formula over a p-adic field, with explicit comparison of the geometric and representation-theoretic objects. This preprint first appeared 10 October 2025 and was revised 28 February 2026; it does not claim priority over all prior proofs. Its \S1.4 credits \citet[Theorem 5.4(b)]{solleveld2021graded} and the standard-module comparison \citet[Lemma 6.2]{solleveld2023submodules}. The two Solleveld preprints first circulated in June 2021 and September 2023; the relevant revisions include May 2022 and May 2024. Both current versions were checked, including January 2025 and April 2026 revisions respectively. The notebook's unspecific remark is therefore a partial match to this precise solved problem; the 1985 Schubert map alone does not prove the representation-theoretic equality.''',
 ['zelevinsky1981padic','balodis2025padic','solleveld2021graded','solleveld2023submodules'],cls='B',match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1981-04',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The original and modern standard-module conventions are kept separate. The group GL(n), cuspidal-line reduction and stalk formula come from the identified 1981 article, not from the notebook line itself.',area='p-adic and geometric representation theory',status='As of November 2026, the identified GL(n) multiplicity statement is proved, with direct current theorem-level sources. No claim is made that all p-adic Kazhdan--Lusztig variants are settled, or that the first proof appeared in 2025.')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1981-04',title='The type-A quiver formula for p-adic GL(n) multiplicities',statement=r'''For standard induced representations of \(GL_n(F)\) on a fixed cuspidal line, express their irreducible Jordan--H\"older multiplicities through local intersection cohomology of the corresponding graded nilpotent/type-A quiver orbit closures. The notebook occurrence is an abbreviated allusion, identified through Zelevinsky's 1981 paper.''',occurrences=[padic_occ],status=r'''As of November 2026: proved in the specified GL(n) setting. Current direct sources are Solleveld's Theorem 5.4(b), his standard-module comparison Lemma 6.2, and Balodis's version-2 Theorem 3.9. First circulation and revision dates are separated in the occurrence. GP-1979-41 concerns the earlier Verma-module/Schubert Kazhdan--Lusztig problem; it is a related precursor, not this p-adic multiplicity question.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=['zelevinsky1981padic','solleveld2021graded','solleveld2023submodules','balodis2025padic']))

item(535,'P000990','construction','A single arrow and determinantal varieties',
 r'''For \(V_1\xrightarrow{A_1}V_2\), determinantal varieties arise.''',
 r'''For \(\dim V_1=p\), \(\dim V_2=q\), the \(GL(V_1)\times GL(V_2)\)-orbits in \(\operatorname{Hom}(V_1,V_2)\) are the rank strata \(\mathcal O_r\), \(0\le r\le\min(p,q)\). Their closures are \(D_r=\{A:\operatorname{rank}A\le r\}\), cut out by the \((r+1)\)-minors, of dimension \(r(p+q-r)\). Thus the general composition-rank description reduces to the ordinary rank invariant. The open rank-r orbit is smooth, but its lower-rank boundary can be singular.''',
 r'''This is the single-arrow special case in \citet[Introduction and \S2]{abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry}. The dimension follows by choosing the image \(r\)-plane and a surjection onto it, giving \(r(q-r)+pr\). The geometric multiplicity calculations on determinantal varieties also occur in \citet[\S3]{zelevinsky1981padic}; that relation is more precise than identifying ordinary rank strata with affine cells.''',
 ['abeasisdelfrakraft1981geometry','zelevinsky1981padic'],literal='correct',area='Quiver representations and algebraic geometry')

