talk(536,'1981-10-19','A. A. Kirillov','P000989-P000993','Rank-two Serre presentations, free Lie dimensions and interlacing arrays',r'''Kirillov asks about the two-generator Lie algebra with two Serre relations, gives the multigraded Witt formula before relations, identifies a Catalan specialization, and passes to the enveloping algebra and a Hilbert-series logarithm. A bare lexicographic word list does not specify a reduction algorithm. Gelfand then asks for the number of integer interlacing arrays with four fixed top entries. The latter counts a basis of one irreducible representation, not distinct representations of that highest weight. The record ends on P000991; the next meeting starts on P000992.''')

item(536,'P000990','question','The two-generator Serre algebra and its dimension table',
 r'''For \(a,b\in\mathbb N\), ask about the Lie algebra generated by \(x,y\), with \((\operatorname{ad}x)^{a+1}y=(\operatorname{ad}y)^{b+1}x=0\). The table has dimensions \(3,4,6,\infty\), but question marks for \((a,b)=(3,2),(2,3)\).''',
 r'''Over characteristic zero and for positive integers \(a,b\), the presented algebra is the positive nilpotent subalgebra of the symmetrizable rank-two Kac--Moody algebra with Cartan matrix \(A=\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}2&-a\\-b&2\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)\). Its total dimension is \(3\) for \(ab=1\), \(4\) for \(ab=2\), \(6\) for \(ab=3\), and infinite for \(ab\ge4\). The finite cases are \(A_2,B_2,G_2\), with those numbers of positive roots; \(ab=4\) is affine, and \(ab>4\) is indefinite. In particular both question marks in the table equal infinity.''',
 r'''The classification and Serre presentation were available before 1981; compare \citet[Chapter II, \S\S4--7]{kac1968graded} and the contemporaneous summary \citet[\S\S1--3]{macdonald1981affine}. The rank-two matrix is symmetrizable by \(D=\operatorname{diag}(b,a)\), and its symmetrized determinant has sign \(4-ab\), directly checking the boundary. The question marks are unresolved entries in these notes, not evidence of an open rank-two classification problem.''',
 ['kac1968graded','macdonald1981affine'],reconstruction='The positive-integer convention and identification with the positive Serre subalgebra are explicit; the dimensions concern that subalgebra, not the full Kac--Moody algebra.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Lie algebras and combinatorics')

item(536,'P000990','theorem','The bigraded Witt formula for a free Lie algebra',
 r'''Without the two relations, the number of commutators with \(k\) occurrences of \(x\) and \(\ell\) of \(y\) is given by a M\"obius sum.''',
 r'''For the free Lie algebra \(L(x,y)\) over a field of characteristic zero, and nonnegative integers \(k,\ell\) with \(k+\ell>0\), \[\dim L_{k,\ell}=\frac1{k+\ell}\sum_{d\mid\gcd(k,\ell)}\mu(d)\binom{(k+\ell)/d}{k/d}.\] The expression counts independent Lie elements, not all formal bracketings. It gives \(\dim L_{1,0}=\dim L_{0,1}=1\) and zero in pure-generator degrees above one. It does not compute the quotient dimensions after imposing Serre relations without further subtraction or reduction.''',
 r'''This is the classical multigraded Witt formula; \citet[proof of Theorem 2 and the free-Lie specialization, pp.~225--226]{bermanmoody1979multiplicities} derives the relevant logarithmic/M\"obius inversion before this seminar. In two letters it also counts primitive necklaces: M\"obius inversion removes proper repetitions, and each primitive necklace has \(k+\ell\) distinct cyclic rotations. The included finite enumeration checks that independent route against the formula.''',
 ['bermanmoody1979multiplicities'],literal='correct',area='Lie algebras and combinatorics')

item(536,'P000990','theorem','The Catalan specialization and the missing product length',
 r'''Set \(k=n,\ell=n+1\); the notes identify the answer with the nth Catalan number and with parenthesizations of a nonassociative product, but do not write its number of factors.''',
 r'''For \(n\ge0\), coprimality gives \[\dim L_{n,n+1}=\frac1{2n+1}\binom{2n+1}{n}=\frac1{n+1}\binom{2n}{n}=C_n.\] The same integer counts full binary parenthesizations of a fixed word of length \(n+1\), rooted plane full binary trees with \(n\) internal vertices, and Dyck words with \(n\) pairs. This equality of counts by itself does not supply a basis of the Serre quotient or a canonical bijection from bracketings to Lie elements.''',
 r'''The assertion is an immediate specialization of the preceding pre-existing Witt formula \citep[pp.~225--226]{bermanmoody1979multiplicities}. The factorial simplification gives one check; the independently implemented Catalan recurrence \(C_0=1\), \(C_n=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}C_jC_{n-1-j}\) gives another. The exact number \(n+1\) of factors is an editorial completion of the ellipsis.''',
 ['bermanmoody1979multiplicities'],reconstruction='The product length and the warning that a counting identity is not a quotient-basis theorem are supplied.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Lie algebras and combinatorics')

item(536,'P000990','construction','The enveloping algebra and alternating associative Serre relations',
 r'''The next question concerns the enveloping algebra. The two iterated brackets are expanded as alternating binomial sums of associative words.''',
 r'''If \(\mathfrak l=L(x,y)/(r_x,r_y)\) with \(r_x=(\operatorname{ad}x)^{a+1}y\), \(r_y=(\operatorname{ad}y)^{b+1}x\), then \[U(\mathfrak l)=\mathbb C\langle x,y\rangle\big/\left(\sum_{j=0}^{a+1}(-1)^j\binom{a+1}{j}x^{a+1-j}yx^j,\ \sum_{j=0}^{b+1}(-1)^j\binom{b+1}{j}y^{b+1-j}xy^j\right).\] These are two-sided ideal generators. The PBW theorem identifies the associated graded algebra for the PBW filtration with \(S(\mathfrak l)\); for the original positive root grading their Hilbert series also agree. It does not say that arbitrary unreduced words in \(x,y\) are a basis.''',
 r'''This is the universal property of enveloping algebras applied to a Lie presentation, together with PBW; the same Serre/PBW setting is reviewed in \citet[\S2]{macdonald1981affine}. The complete signs and upper limits here expand the notebook's ellipses rather than emend its two visible signs. The binomial identity follows because left and right multiplication by \(x\) commute, so \(\operatorname{ad}x=L_x-R_x\).''',
 ['macdonald1981affine'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Lie algebras and combinatorics')

item(536,'P000990-P000991','theorem','The symmetric-algebra Euler transform and divisor sums',
 r'''For a graded \(V\), the source first gives \(p(t)=\sum\dim V_k t^k\), then \(\log P(t)=\sum C_k t^k/k\), \(p(t)=\sum c_k t^k/k\), and \(C_k=\sum_{d\mid k}c_d\). The denominator in the latter expression for \(p\) is clearly written in the scan.''',
 r'''Assume \(V=\bigoplus_{k\ge1}V_k\) and every \(v_k=\dim V_k\) is finite. Then \[P(t)=\operatorname{Hilb}S(V)=\prod_{k\ge1}(1-t^k)^{-v_k},\qquad [t^n]\log P(t)=\frac1n\sum_{d\mid n}d v_d.\] Thus with \(c_k=kv_k\), the two notebook formulas for \(p(t)\) agree and \(C_n=\sum_{d\mid n}c_d\). Inversion is \(v_n=n^{-1}\sum_{d\mid n}\mu(d)C_{n/d}\). All identities are formal power-series identities; a nonzero degree-zero piece would invalidate finite coefficients of \(P(t)\).''',
 r'''This elementary Euler-transform computation is the commutative/PBW mechanism behind the logarithm and M\"obius inversion in \citet[proof of Theorem 2]{bermanmoody1979multiplicities}. In particular the source's \(c_k/k\) should \emph{not} be corrected to \(c_k\): its coefficient names have been rescaled. The notebook itself already allows a formal series after initially saying `Hilbert polynomial'.''',
 ['bermanmoody1979multiplicities'],reconstruction='Positive grading, finite-dimensional pieces and the rescaled coefficient convention make the compressed formulas consistent.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Lie algebras and combinatorics')

item(536,'P000991','method','The uncompleted lexicographic word method',
 r'''Words \(x^{k_1}y^{\ell_1}\cdots x^{k_s}y^{\ell_s}\), their two total degrees, and lexicographic order \(x<y\) are written, without a stated selection, bracketing or reduction rule.''',
 r'''No unique algorithm is specified. For a free Lie algebra, a possible completion would select Lyndon words and apply their standard recursive bracketing; for the Serre quotient one would additionally need reductions by the ideal, with a proof of spanning and independence. A total order on all associative words alone provides neither step, and the notebook does not state a termination or confluence claim.''',
 r'''The preceding free-Lie dimension and enveloping-algebra discussion makes a Hall/Lyndon-type idea plausible, but neither the exact word selection nor a quotient normal-form theorem can be identified from the surviving three lines. Berman--Moody's multiplicity formula is only thematic context and does not identify the omitted algorithm. No modern basis construction is reported as a solution of an unspecified method.''',[],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',reconstruction='Lyndon bracketing is an explicitly labelled possible completion, not an attribution to Kirillov.',area='Lie algebras and combinatorics',status='As of November 2026, the precise proposed algorithm remains unidentified from the notes; no well-posed unresolved basis conjecture is extracted.')

gt_occ=item(536,'P000991','problem','Counting interlacing tables with four fixed top entries',
 r'''Gelfand fixes \(m_1\le m_2\le m_3\le m_4\), asks how many integer triangular tables have each entry between its two upper neighbours including endpoints, and parenthetically calls this the number of representations of \(SL(4)\) with a given highest weight.''',
 r'''For fixed integers \(m_1\le\cdots\le m_4\), let \(a_{4,i}=m_i\) and impose \(a_{r+1,i}\le a_{r,i}\le a_{r+1,i+1}\) for \(1\le i\le r<4\). The number is \[N(m)=\prod_{1\le i<j\le4}\frac{m_j-m_i+j-i}{j-i}.\] Equivalently, \[\begin{aligned}12N(m)={}&(m_2-m_1+1)(m_3-m_1+2)(m_4-m_1+3)\\ &{}\cdot(m_3-m_2+1)(m_4-m_2+2)(m_4-m_3+1).\end{aligned}\] It is the dimension of the irreducible \(GL_4\)-module of highest weight \((m_4,m_3,m_2,m_1)\), or its irreducible restriction to \(SL_4\), because the patterns index a basis. There is one isomorphism class of irreducible representation for a fixed dominant integral \(SL_4\) highest weight; that number is not generally \(N(m)\).''',
 r'''\citet[\S3, formula (3), reprint p.~655]{gelfandtsetlin1950unimodular} gives precisely the arbitrary-rank integer interlacing basis; its original article was submitted 16 February 1950 and published that year. The product is the finite type-A Weyl dimension specialization \citep[VII.8]{serre1987complex}. The notebook itself points to the Gelfand--Minlos--Shapiro appendix, but this identification is verified against the earlier primary paper. Direct recursive enumeration and the six-factor product agree for all top rows in the bounded computation supplied.''',
 ['gelfandtsetlin1950unimodular','serre1987complex'],literal='false as written',problem='GP-1981-03',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Combinatorics and finite-dimensional representations',reconstruction='Integrality of every entry, reversal to the usual decreasing highest-weight convention, and the distinction between a basis count and a count of representations are explicit.',status='As of November 2026, this is a classical solved counting problem. The literal parenthetical identification in the notebook is false, although the intended dimension interpretation is exact.')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1981-03',title='Integer Gelfand–Tsetlin arrays with four fixed top entries',statement=r'''Count integer triangular interlacing arrays with fixed weakly increasing top row \(m_1,m_2,m_3,m_4\), including equality at every interlacing bound.''',occurrences=[gt_occ],status=r'''As of November 2026: solved by the type-A Weyl dimension product displayed in 1981-536.7 and the 1950 Gelfand--Tsetlin basis. The earlier 1976-415.5 and GP-1976-24 concern standard tableaux/Young-graph paths, a related but different branching graph; no exact recurrence of this fixed-integer-row question is present in the supplied index.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=['gelfandtsetlin1950unimodular','serre1987complex']))

