talk(537,'1981-11-16','A. S. Schwarz','P000991-P000995','Supergravity as the geometry of a real supersurface',r'''Schwarz treats real \((4|4)\)-dimensional supersurfaces in \(\mathbb C^{4|2}\), a holomorphic volume-preserving pseudogroup, the odd complex tangent distribution and its Levi form. The action is written as a delta-function surface integral; a preliminary exponent on P000993 is explicitly replaced by the Jacobian-corrected expression on P000994. An SCR structure retains a determinant normalization absent from ordinary CR data. The October 1981 announcement is identified bibliographically, and the detailed formulas are verified in \citet[\S3, pp.~44--48]{schwarz1982supergravity}, received 15 February 1982. The broader field-space-democracy work is not confused with this exact Levi determinant formula. The record ends before the 23 November heading on P000995.''')

item(537,'P000992','definition','Superspace as a functor of Grassmann algebras',
 r'''A real superspace is a row of even \(x^i\) and odd \(\theta^\alpha\) elements of a Grassmann algebra. The speaker insists on varying the algebra functorially instead of fixing it.''',
 r'''For a finite-dimensional real Grassmann algebra \(\Lambda=\Lambda_{\bar0}\oplus\Lambda_{\bar1}\), affine superspace has \(\mathbb R^{p|q}(\Lambda)=\Lambda_{\bar0}^{p}\times\Lambda_{\bar1}^{q}\). An even unital algebra homomorphism acts componentwise, giving a covariant functor of test algebras. Smooth supermaps are the natural maps induced by smooth functions in the even body variables and finite exterior polynomials in odd coordinates, with the appropriate smoothness structure on the functor. An arbitrary set-valued functor is not automatically a smooth supermanifold. Complexification introduces conjugation as additional real-structure data.''',
 r'''The local superfunction and integration framework predates the meeting: \citet[\S2, Definitions 1--4; \S3, Theorem 1]{berezinleites1975supermanifolds}, received 24 March 1975, was read in the original. Schwarz's exact functorial presentation is explained in \citet[\S1]{schwarz1982supergravity}. These are equivalent ways to organize the local model, but the 1975 sheaf definition is not falsely quoted as the same wording as the later functor exposition.''',
 ['berezinleites1975supermanifolds','schwarz1982supergravity'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Supergeometry and mathematical physics')

item(537,'P000992-P000993','construction','A real (4|4) supersurface and its odd complex tangent space',
 r'''Four real even equations cut out \(\Omega\subset\mathbb C^{4,2}\), of real dimension \((4,4)\). Solving \(f^k_{x^a}dx^a+f^k_{\theta^\alpha}d\theta^\alpha=0\) gives a complex tangent space of dimension \((0,2)\).''',
 r'''Let \(z=(x^1,\ldots,x^4,\theta^1,\theta^2)\) be complex supercoordinates. Four real even functions \(f^k(z,\bar z)\) with independent real even body differentials define a real supersubmanifold of dimension \((4|4)\). On a generic chart assume additionally that the even \(4\times4\) matrix \(f_x=(\partial f^k/\partial x^a)\) has invertible body. Then \(T^{1,0}\Omega=\ker\partial f\) is locally free of rank \((0|2)\), with frame \[R_\alpha=\partial_{\theta^\alpha}-\sum_a(f_x^{-1}f_\theta)^a{}_{\alpha}\,\partial_{x^a}.\] Real regularity alone does not imply this complex genericity condition.''',
 r'''The regular-level-set mechanism is already \citet[\S4, Theorem 2]{berezinleites1975supermanifolds}; the generic supersurface and its complex tangent plane are treated explicitly in \citet[\S3, equations (1)--(3), pp.~44--46]{schwarz1982supergravity}. The notebook's linear elimination is valid on precisely this invertible chart. The dimension \((0|2)\) describes the tangent distribution, not the rank or dimension of the four-component Levi form.''',
 ['berezinleites1975supermanifolds','schwarz1982supergravity'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The real regularity and separate invertibility of the even complex Jacobian are supplied. The explicit frame is obtained by the elimination written in the notes.',area='Supergeometry and mathematical physics')

item(537,'P000992-P000993','definition','Holomorphic transformations preserving the Berezin volume',
 r'''Holomorphic coordinate transformations form \(L\); the notes then restrict to those satisfying \(\operatorname{Ber}(\partial\widetilde z/\partial z)=1\), and later again call the symmetry group \(L\).''',
 r'''Use \(L_{\mathrm{vol}}\) for the local holomorphic invertible coordinate changes preserving the chosen nonvanishing holomorphic Berezin volume. In a volume coordinate chart they satisfy \(\operatorname{Ber}J=1\). For an even block supermatrix \(J=\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}A&B\\C&D\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)\), with \(D\) invertible, \(\operatorname{Ber}J=\det(A-BD^{-1}C)/\det D\). Its multiplicativity gives a consistent transformation law for Berezin integration. All local holomorphic transformations form a larger pseudogroup; a fixed-domain global group requires additional domain conditions.''',
 r'''The Berezinian density rule is explicit in \citet[\S3, equation (3) and Theorem 1]{berezinleites1975supermanifolds}. The specific supergravity pseudogroup is used in \citet[\S3]{schwarz1982supergravity}. The passage from all holomorphic transformations to the volume-preserving subgroup is present in the source; the later symbol reuse is the ambiguity, not the determinant condition itself.''',
 ['berezinleites1975supermanifolds','schwarz1982supergravity'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The volume-preserving subgroup notation separates the two successive meanings of L without changing the intended volume symmetry.',area='Supergeometry and mathematical physics')

item(537,'P000993-P000994','construction','The graph superfield and field-space democracy',
 r'''The supersurface is put in one-to-one correspondence with a field \(H^m(\xi,\theta,\bar\theta):\mathbb R^{4,4}\to\mathbb R^{4,0}\). The closing remark says that coordinates and field values mix under the ambient transformations.''',
 r'''Write complex even coordinates locally as \(x^m=\xi^m+i\eta^m\). If projection of \(\Omega\) to \((\xi,\theta,\bar\theta)\) is locally invertible, it has a unique graph \(\eta^m=H^m(\xi,\theta,\bar\theta)\). This is a local equivalence in a chosen projection chart; it is not a global statement for every embedded supersurface. A holomorphic transformation of \((x,\theta)\) can mix \(\xi\) and \(H\), so the field transforms by re-expressing the transformed surface as a graph wherever that projection remains regular.''',
 r'''The geometric reformulation is part of Schwarz's earlier field-space-democracy program, bibliographically identified as \citet{schwarz1980equal}. The graph construction and its supergravity meaning are read directly in \citet[\S3]{schwarz1982supergravity}; the 1980 article itself was not read. This is a classical local implicit-function argument within the stated supergeometric model, not a claim of a global trivialization of all possible field configurations.''',
 ['schwarz1980equal','schwarz1982supergravity'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',reconstruction='The projection condition, real/imaginary decomposition and local scope are supplied; the invariance concerns the surface description.',area='Supergeometry and mathematical physics')

item(537,'P000993-P000994','construction','The vector-valued Levi form and its four-by-four determinant',
 r'''Restrict \(\partial_A\partial_{\bar B}f^k\,dz^A d\bar z^B\) to the complex tangent plane. Its components are \(\Gamma^k_{\alpha\dot\beta}\); the index \(k\) has four values and the paired spinor index has \(2\times2=4\).''',
 r'''On the generic chart of 1981-537.2, evaluate the mixed Hessian of each \(f^k\) on \(R_\alpha,\overline{R_\beta}\), with a fixed convention for odd derivatives and any conventional overall factor of \(i\). This is the normal-valued Levi tensor. In the chosen normal and odd tangent frames it is a \(4\times4\) matrix \(\Gamma=(\Gamma^k_{\alpha\dot\beta})\), after ordering the four pairs \((\alpha,\dot\beta)\). Its determinant is a relative invariant of the frames, not a scalar independent of them. The supergravity density discussed next is defined on the locus where the body of this determinant is nonzero; fractional powers need a consistent real/complex branch.''',
 r'''\citet[\S3, equations (3)--(8), pp.~45--47]{schwarz1982supergravity} defines the Levi form, tracks changes of defining equations and tangent frame, and constructs the compensating invariant density. The October 1981 announcement \citep{schwarz1981complex} is an earlier identified publication in this line, but only its metadata was checked. Classification A refers to the detailed post-seminar treatment, not a claim that the Levi form itself originated in 1982.''',
 ['schwarz1982supergravity','schwarz1981complex'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Invertibility, the paired-index ordering and branch conventions are supplied. Changing the conventional Levi factor changes only an overall normalization of the action.',area='Supergeometry and mathematical physics',status='As of November 2026, the construction and covariance law are explicit in the identified primary paper. The notebook does not determine a numerical choice of overall Levi normalization.')

item(537,'P000993-P000994','theorem','The invariant action density and the corrected exponents',
 r'''P000993 first writes \(A=|\det\Gamma|^{-1/2}\). P000994 then says \ru{и тогда надо писать} and gives \(A=(\det f_z)^{4/3}|\det\Gamma|^{-1/3}\). Both exponents and the added Jacobian are visible in the scans.''',
 r'''For a regular generic supersurface on the nondegenerate Levi locus, the invariant density in the natural odd-coordinate frame is, up to a constant and orientation conventions, \[A=|\det f_x|^{4/3}|\det\Gamma|^{-1/3},\qquad S(\Omega)=C\int A\,\prod_{k=1}^4\delta(f^k)\,Dz\,D\bar z.\] Here \(x^a\) denotes the four complex even coordinates, not all six supercoordinates; \(D\) is Berezin integration notation. Under replacement of equations by \(f'=\eta f\), the two determinants each acquire \(\det\eta\) on the surface, while the four-delta product acquires \(|\det\eta|^{-1}\); the exponents cancel. In a general tangent frame the first factor is Schwarz's compensator \(|W|^{4/3}\). Thus the action depends on the embedded surface and preserved ambient volume, and is invariant under \(L_{\mathrm{vol}}\).''',
 r'''This is \citet[\S3, equations (7)--(8), pp.~46--47]{schwarz1982supergravity}, received 15 February 1982; the author gives the defining-equation and tangent-frame covariance checks. The action had been announced in the October 1981 \emph{Yadernaya Fizika} paper \citep{schwarz1981complex}. The initial minus-one-half formula is not used as a second valid expression: under \(f\mapsto cf\) it has the wrong scaling in the displayed natural frame. The published formula also supplies absolute values/real-density conventions missing on the first determinant in the notebook.''',
 ['schwarz1982supergravity','schwarz1981complex'],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='exact',reconstruction='The first determinant is explicitly the even complex Jacobian; absolute values or an equivalent oriented-branch convention are supplied. The change of formula inside the notebook is retained as evidence of an internal correction.',area='Supergeometry and mathematical physics',status='As of November 2026, the corrected first/second-derivative action density is established in the cited primary source. The preliminary minus-one-half density is false under the explicitly chosen frames and equation-change covariance.')

item(537,'P000993','claim','Uniqueness within the stated first/second-derivative action class',
 r'''The action is said to be the unique functional of the displayed kind, up to a multiplicative constant, invariant under \(L\). The following line about variation is unfinished and gives no field equation.''',
 r'''The precise identified claim restricts to local actions of generic nondegenerate \((4|4)\)-supersurfaces whose density depends on their defining functions through first and second derivatives, is independent of the choice of defining equations, and is invariant under holomorphic transformations preserving the ambient Berezin volume. Within that class the source asserts uniqueness up to a constant. At the determinant-monomial level, writing \(A=|W|^p|\Gamma|^q\), equation covariance and tangent-frame covariance impose \(p+q=1\) and \(p+4q=0\), hence \(p=4/3,q=-1/3\). This elementary exponent calculation does not, by itself, classify all admissible local functions of the jets.''',
 r'''\citet[\S3, p.~48, following equation (9)]{schwarz1982supergravity} explicitly states the restricted uniqueness result, and at p.~58 refers its proof to \citet{gaydukromanovschwarz1981democracy}. The latter's bibliographic record and abstract were verified, but its proof was inaccessible in this search; therefore the full uniqueness proof is recorded as indirect verification. The 1982 paper's symmetry and determinant calculations were read. No uniqueness theorem for arbitrary derivative orders, extra matter fields or other supergravity models is inferred, and no Euler--Lagrange system is reconstructed from the unfinished sentence.''',
 ['schwarz1982supergravity','gaydukromanovschwarz1981democracy'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The derivative-order and geometric domain restrictions come from the identified paper. The two-exponent check is editorial; the stronger classification argument has not been independently read.',area='Supergeometry and mathematical physics',status='As of November 2026, the exact published uniqueness claim is identified with its stated action class; its original full proof remains an access limitation of this audit, not an open mathematical problem.')

item(537,'P000994','construction','SCR structure as CR data with ambient-volume normalization',
 r'''A complex G-structure induces a CR structure on the surface. Here the ambient group is written \(SL(4,2\mid\mathbb C)\), and the induced SCR structure differs from CR by a determinant condition.''',
 r'''On a complex \((4|2)\)-supermanifold with a chosen holomorphic Berezin volume, the compatible ambient frames form an \(SL(4|2;\mathbb C)\)-reduction, where `SL' means Berezinian one. A generic real \((4|4)\)-supersurface inherits its odd complex tangent CR distribution together with the additional frame normalization determined by that volume. This is Schwarz's SCR structure. Forgetting the normalization yields the CR data; retaining it makes the determinant-compensated action intrinsic to the induced structure. The local automorphisms preserve both the holomorphic structure and the chosen volume.''',
 r'''\citet[\S\S4--6, especially the SCR frame construction in \S6]{schwarz1982supergravity} gives the detailed G-structure formulation soon after the seminar. \citet[\S\S1--3]{konechnyschwarz1997geometry}, first circulated in September 1996 and published in 1997, subsequently uses these SCR superspaces for \(N=1\) super Yang--Mills theory in curved superspace. That is a concrete later application, not a purported first proof of the 1981 construction or a solution of an unstated conjecture.''',
 ['schwarz1982supergravity','konechnyschwarz1997geometry'],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',reconstruction='The modern vertical-bar notation and the distinction between ordinary determinant and Berezinian are supplied; the role of a fixed ambient volume is made explicit.',area='Supergeometry and mathematical physics',status='As of November 2026, the SCR/G-structure construction and the cited later gauge-theory application are identified. The brief source does not assert a classification of all abstract SCR structures or a global embedding theorem.')

